They first drill around the truck and carefully plant dynamite to blast it out.

451
00:38:07,920 --> 00:38:19,080
It's a bit of a risky process, but again, we try and calculate our risk and make sure that we use a lot of caution while we're doing this.

452
00:38:20,900 --> 00:38:28,080
Once the tanker is securely harnessed to the salvage equipment, the crew lights the fuses and blasts the monster truck free.

453
00:38:37,920 --> 00:38:42,500
Slowly, inch by inch, the tanker is hauled out of the freezing water.

454
00:38:44,760 --> 00:38:54,060
It took Robinson and his team one week, working in savage minus 40 degree weather, to recover this Super B train and save its lethal cargo.

455
00:38:56,180 --> 00:39:00,720
RTO charged the fuel carrier an estimated $60,000 for the recovery.

456
00:39:02,220 --> 00:39:05,840
The rogue driver was fined thousands of dollars for his mistake.

457
00:39:05,841 --> 00:39:12,120
Over the years, we've become very, very good at being able to salvage equipment.

458
00:39:12,121 --> 00:39:17,840
We've never had a unit that's gone all the way to the bottom that we've never recovered.

459
00:39:19,720 --> 00:39:23,220
However, if a truck does break through and sink to the bottom,

460
00:39:23,800 --> 00:39:26,180
a special Arctic dive unit is called in.

461
00:39:29,640 --> 00:39:33,360
A lot of the recovery work that we do is an instant kind of thing.

462
00:39:33,440 --> 00:39:34,420
It happens over the phone.

463
00:39:34,421 --> 00:39:37,500
a sergeant from a detachment will phone us and say,

464
00:39:37,940 --> 00:39:41,760
Wayne, we have a problem here, we've got two people under the ice.

465
00:39:43,860 --> 00:39:48,040
Typically, by the time Wayne Zowski and his Arctic dive team are deployed,

466
00:39:48,720 --> 00:39:51,380
the lake is again iced over where the vehicle fell through.

467
00:39:53,140 --> 00:39:56,260
They choose a drop zone, set up a work site,

468
00:39:56,720 --> 00:39:58,780
and then cut a hole big enough for the diver.

469
00:39:59,580 --> 00:40:04,040
You learn how to really, you know, work with impossible odds sometimes,

470
00:40:04,041 --> 00:40:08,080
But after a while, it becomes very, very second to your nature.

471
00:40:08,420 --> 00:40:11,180
And that's part of the, quote, Arctic experience.

472
00:40:11,600 --> 00:40:18,080
If you allow frustrations to happen, or you allow Mother Nature to get the best of you,

473
00:40:18,420 --> 00:40:21,060
then that type of work can become very dangerous.

474
00:40:24,340 --> 00:40:26,440
Thankfully, fatalities are rare.

475
00:40:27,320 --> 00:40:29,800
Fewer than a dozen drivers have perished on the lakes.

476
00:40:29,801 --> 00:40:34,120
The last fatality was more than 10 years ago.

477
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:40,880
As this mighty convoy of tanks was crossing Great Slave Lake,

478
00:40:41,460 --> 00:40:45,360
the lead driver, or lead hen, found a fracture in the ice.

479
00:40:46,720 --> 00:40:49,340
This fracture is known as a pressure ridge.

480
00:40:49,900 --> 00:40:52,700
It occurs when there are sudden changes in the temperature.

481
00:40:53,700 --> 00:40:57,580
As ice freezes, it pushes together, creating bumps or ridges.

482
00:40:58,460 --> 00:41:05,140
Then, as it thaws, it pulls apart and opens up dangerous cracks, fissures in the ice.

483
00:41:07,520 --> 00:41:11,900
If drivers can't cross a pressure ridge, they will have to find a way around it.

484
00:41:11,901 --> 00:41:19,420
You can see the water, and the crack is only about eight inches wide, and it goes straight down.

485
00:41:19,820 --> 00:41:24,360
And the ice is probably four or five feet deep here.

486
00:41:24,361 --> 00:41:28,120
and I only guess at the depth of the water,

487
00:41:28,220 --> 00:41:32,520
but it could be anywhere from 50 to 150 feet deep.

488
00:41:34,180 --> 00:41:39,080
Like a plate of glass, one crack and a whole lake of ice can shatter.

489
00:41:39,420 --> 00:41:42,220
The thicker the glass, the more weight it'll support,

490
00:41:42,660 --> 00:41:44,880
and the ice is very, very similar to that.

491
00:41:44,881 --> 00:41:49,160
So what we need to do is monitor how we're putting our roads in

492
00:41:49,161 --> 00:41:51,300
and make sure that as we operate,

493
00:41:51,301 --> 00:41:54,460
We're not running into any of the crack areas.

494
00:41:56,460 --> 00:42:02,760
In a lesson gleaned from the pioneer ice truckers, RTL is prepared for any contingency.

495
00:42:04,380 --> 00:42:09,640
This time, a plow truck is deployed to forge a new road 50 yards to the east.

496
00:42:11,600 --> 00:42:15,440
However, on this new road, another ice fracture is found.

497
00:42:15,441 --> 00:42:23,300
This time, they deploy a portable steel bridge, specifically designed to allow these giant

498
00:42:23,301 --> 00:42:26,840
rigs to cross widening fissures.

499
00:42:26,841 --> 00:42:33,620
It's also quicker than plodding and plowing a new rope.

500
00:42:33,621 --> 00:42:37,480
The drivers unchain the bridges and lay them over the crack.

501
00:42:37,481 --> 00:42:42,000
Then each load is carefully and painstakingly guided over the narrow bridge.

502
00:42:42,001 --> 00:42:44,680
They cannot afford to make a mistake here.

503
00:42:49,240 --> 00:42:54,940
The drivers and their five tanks cross the 150-mile-long Great Slave Lake intact,

504
00:42:55,820 --> 00:43:00,720
safely reaching Yellowknife, just as John Dennison did in 1947.

505
00:43:03,120 --> 00:43:08,720
It's the unique thrill of ice road driving that draws this highly skilled team of truckers.

506
00:43:08,721 --> 00:43:16,580
another reason drivers are lured here season after season is the spectacular even magical

507
00:43:16,581 --> 00:43:24,620
environment they encounter including the mythical northern lights you could almost sit there and

508
00:43:24,621 --> 00:43:29,300
touch it but you can't and it's just and it's changing all the time i sat there probably for

509
00:43:29,301 --> 00:43:36,100
an hour listening to these northern lights and just watching them it's been a wonderful experience

510
00:43:36,101 --> 00:43:42,200
It's just so much to learn about the way the water works and the principle of hydraulics under the ice

511
00:43:42,201 --> 00:43:46,580
and how you operate your vehicle over it, how you treat the ice.

512
00:43:47,620 --> 00:43:51,680
There's a whole book full of learning to go into a very short space of time.

513
00:43:54,260 --> 00:43:57,880
These truckers follow the footsteps of the great explorers

514
00:43:57,881 --> 00:44:02,220
and are fused with the pioneer spirit of the first ice road truckers.

515
00:44:02,221 --> 00:44:09,220
They carry the vital knowledge of Arctic survival learned from the native peoples of the north.

516
00:44:09,221 --> 00:44:19,700
They push ever on to tear through tundra, break bush, and skate over frozen lakes to carve

517
00:44:19,701 --> 00:44:26,300
roads that melt away with the arrival of spring.

518
00:44:26,301 --> 00:44:32,340
year when the brutal arctic winter bears down and claims this magnificent and spellbinding

519
00:44:32,341 --> 00:44:43,140
land, the best drivers will return again to ride the most challenging roads on earth.

